+## 8. Known gaps
+
+- **The live API has been used by the smoke script only**, twice, on 27 Oct 2026 (section 4 has the numbers). No page has talked to it: `/talk` and the plan card have not been used with the real API in a browser, and no real person's speech has been transcribed. The Billing balance has not been looked at.
+- **The plan card's browser gate is narrow.** S16 to S22 of `talk-ui` drive the card in a real Edge against a fake model: its own consent tick and nothing sent without it, one plan, a plan in each of the 8 languages, every way a request can fail, Cancel, and the 360 px layout. Not covered: a plan that arrives after the answers changed (`app-state-test` covers the stored state, not the browser), the Cancel button's layout on a phone, and the real model's output in a browser. After a failed request the consent tick stays ticked; that is observed, not pinned.
+- **The checks on a written plan are narrow.** Code refuses a plan that has a digit, a number word, a letter of the wrong script, a link, a stray brace, a blank that was not offered, a required blank that is missing, the wrong number of steps or cautions, or (in English) an acronym or a capitalised word. It cannot tell whether the plan is wise, whether a Marathi plan is Marathi and not Hindi, or whether a place or scheme name written in the native script is real, and the lists of number words are not complete for every language. In a plan in another language the bold scheme name is the English one.
+- No native speaker has reviewed any non-English wording, in the interface, in the 50 `talk.*` keys, the 20 `plan.*` keys, the 21 `fo.*` keys, the 10 `rp.schedule*` keys, the 8 egg-price keys, the 24 `fs.*` keys, the 32 `cl.*` keys or the 3 scheme status sentences (`elig.pmfme.upgrade`, `elig.pmegp.period`, `elig.standupindia.period`) added with them (the last eight sets are machine-written). The 5 `amt.*` keys (the figure beside a piece of advice) and the 35 `offer.*` keys (the loan-offer card) are machine-written in 6 languages too (all but English and Hindi); their Hindi was returned as final by the owner on 31 Oct 2026 (7 of the 40 lines changed), one person's check and not a native-speaker review. Have `fs.specialWho` read first: it says who the special category is.
+- The three model routes (`/api/intake/*` and `/api/advisor/plan`) have no authentication, no per-user limit and no server-side consent record. The hourly limit is per process, and the ledger bounds this app on one machine, not the account. A copy hosted on the open internet would let anyone who finds it spend the owner's credits up to the cap, so the plan is a recorded demo and a run-it-yourself README, not a hosted copy. A production frontend build needs an https `VITE_API_BASE_URL`, and a browser allows the microphone only on https or on localhost.
+- A spoken rupee amount is converted by the model and relies on the person's review.
+- No `safety_identifier` is sent with a request, and zero data retention has not been requested. Ask Meta before recordings of real people go through the helper.
+- `/privacy` does not mention the helper or the plan card yet. `docs/PROCESSOR_SCHEDULE.md` section 10 records what a deployer has to add.
+- The market rows for Lucknow and Varanasi are from the fetch of 24 Sept 2026; 11 districts were fetched on 27 Oct and 18 on 29 Oct. A mandi price is the median of the 30 reporting days before its fetch, so refetch before recording the video. **Lucknow's and Varanasi's prices (last reported 19 to 23 Sept) show no "may be out of date" warning**, because that flag is set when the data is fetched, and Lucknow is the demo district: skip the refetch and the video shows five-week-old prices with no warning. Five of the 31 districts (Guntur, Dhubri, East Singhbhum, Ranchi, South 24 Parganas) have business counts and no crop price, and Kanpur Nagar's prices rest on our reading of Agmarknet's name "Kanpur" (`DATA_SOURCES.md`).
+- **The egg prices have three open questions for NECC.** The unit is our reading: NECC prints whole numbers with no unit, and we take 640 to be 640 paise an egg (₹6.40). The permission is an e-mail, and nobody has asked whether NECC's fourth clarification sentence wants more than the clarification printed beside the prices, or whether the agreement reaches a client who deploys the app. The owner wrote on 29 Oct 2026 that the NECC use is confirmed; that message is not on file and what it covered is not recorded, so all three stay open. NECC's own "Average" column disagrees with its daily cells for Kolkata (632.59 against 634.46), which is why the app never reads it. Four of NECC's 34 centres are not mapped (Barwala, East Godavari, West Godavari, Mumbai). The file is one fetch of a daily sheet: run `node tools/fetch-necc-prices.mjs --write` just before recording. A poultry reader in Uttar Pradesh gets 15 schemes, all "For all of India", so the scheme-fit chart (which needs two groups) does not draw.
+- **The funding card rests on three pages, each with a gap, and tests no eligibility.** PMEGP (KVIC's guidelines of 1 June 2022): the approval ran for 2021-22 to 2025-26 and a continuation after 31 March 2026 could not be confirmed (KVIC's own site could not be read to check). PMFME (the Ministry's page, last updated 10 Feb 2021): it is about units that want to upgrade, says nothing about new units and gives no period. Mudra (Department of Financial Services, updated 5 Feb 2026): food processing is matched by our inference from "Non-agricultural". Stand-Up India's period reads "upto 31.03.2025" with a successor announced in the 2025-26 Budget, and its margin figures differ between sources, so the card gives it a status sentence and no sum. The card does not test whether the repayment fits the household, so the bank loan it shows can be larger than the safe-loan cards beside it; it says "estimate, not an offer". Its 24 `fs.*` keys and 3 status sentences are machine-written in 7 languages.
+- **The registrations card rests on two published texts and a reading of ours.** The FSSAI limits come from regulations of 2011 as compiled on 9 Nov 2017 and the GST limits from a CBIC booklet of 1 June 2019, and neither was checked against later amendments: re-verify before a real person acts on the card. The FSSAI definition of a petty business joins its limits with "and/or" (the card says "usually"), and also counts hawkers, itinerant vendors and temporary stall holders whatever their sales (the card does not print that). The booklet gives no rule for a business that sells both goods and services, so the card says the sales are between the two limits and sends the reader to the GST office. Which words make a business food, goods or services is our own table. The ten buttons are covered; something typed under "other" that holds none of the words (livestock, or anything in a non-Latin script) gets "mixed" for GST and "depends" for FSSAI, which only ever asks the reader to check. The Udyam line counts only the two matched schemes that say Udyam is required (Kerala and North-East). The card's 32 `cl.*` keys are machine-written in 7 languages.
+- **The figure beside a piece of advice is one number per sentence and no more.** Only six sentences have one (section 2.2). `/advisory` and `/report` show only the first five recommendations, as they did before, so a figure that belongs to a sixth is not shown there, and the next step's figure shows on `/analysis` only. The cash reserve of ₹10,000 is the app's own mark, as it always was. The figure is a limit the page's own pressure and household checks agree with, found by searching over whole rupees; it is not a recommendation to borrow up to it.
+- **The loan-offer card has met a browser gate and no person.** `tools/verify/offer-ui.mjs` types offers into it in a real Edge, in all 8 languages, against a stored result with no backend, and fails if a request or a stored value appears. Nobody has asked a borrower or a field worker whether the two ways of telling an offer match how offers are really told to them. Its yearly cost is a nominal rate (payments in a year times the rate for one payment, no interest on interest), which is not any lender's APR, and the card never calls it one. For payments every day it counts 365 a year, so the figure is too high when the lender does not collect every day (the card says so). A fee taken out of a loan told as a rate is not counted (the card says that too). The comparison at 12% a year is the rate this app assumes, not a quote. A number typed with a comma, a sign or a letter is refused, not converted; digits other than 0 to 9 (Devanagari, for example) are refused the same way, and the Hindi message does not say so. This card is the one place where a figure is worked out in the browser and not in Python; step 4 of the form also adds up the funding gap and the monthly surplus in the tab, as a running summary that the result does not use.
+- A recording that is too short to decode is reported with `talk.errRecording`, whose wording ("speak for less than a minute") fits a recording that is too long, not one that is too short.
+- `Retry-After` is not exposed to the browser through CORS, so the page shows a generic "busy" message.
